<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Entitlements required to distribute MIT/GNU Scheme on macOS with the
    hardened runtime (which notarization requires).

    The native-code system executes compiled Scheme out of the heap.  On
    AArch64 the heap is mapped twice: writable at the canonical
    addresses, and read/execute in a shadow alias made with
    mach_vm_remap.  The process therefore creates executable pages that
    Apple did not sign, and under the hardened runtime that requires
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit is NOT sufficient, and is not used:
    it governs MAP_JIT regions, whose write-protect toggle is
    per-thread and exclusive, and so cannot support a heap that
    interleaves code with data that the running code allocates.
    Verified empirically: under the hardened runtime a binary carrying
    only allow-jit fails to start, while one carrying
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory runs normally.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
